## 2. Anti-pattern catalog — name it, kill it, rebuild it

### 2.1 The "Not X. It is Y." hammer
**Bad:** "It is not a static layer but a dynamic field of personified forces." / "This is not metaphor." / "Not the laboratory kind, but the inner work."
**Why it's slop:** overused, it stops being a rhetorical correction and becomes a tic — the model reaching for gravitas instead of earning it.
**Rebuild:** assert directly, or invert into an image instead of a negation.
> "This middle realm churns. Corbin fills it with gods, angels, daimons — not furniture, but forces."
**Rule:** one "not X, but Y" per 800–1000 words, max. If you've used it once this section, the next correction needs a different shape (see 3.3).

### 2.2 Narrating your own moves
**Bad:** "Voss opens with an unexpected angle." "Voss closes with an image that ties everything together." "This brings us to the next point."
**Why it's slop:** it's the voice of a summarizer describing a paper, not a writer with a stake in the idea. It also wastes a sentence on scaffolding instead of content.
**Rebuild:** delete the narration, deliver the content in first-person authority.
> Bad: "Voss opens with an unexpected angle: alchemy."
> Good: "Corbin's alchemy has nothing to do with gold. It's the soul learning to see through itself."
**Rule:** if a sentence's subject is the essay/author/argument rather than the actual idea, cut it or fold it into a stronger claim.

### 2.3 The three-beat list
**Bad:** "theophanic visions, dreams, meditative states, artistic inspiration, romantic love." / "travelling to the underworld, fighting the dragon, undergoing the alchemical nigredo."
**Why it's slop:** this cadence — comma, comma, comma, stop — is one of the single most recognizable LLM fingerprints. It performs thoroughness without giving any item weight.
**Rebuild:** pick one item, go deep on it, mention the others in passing or not at all. Or break the parallel grammar so the list doesn't scan evenly.
> "Corbin's imaginal world admits dreams, yes, and visions — but also, oddly, romantic love, which he treats with the same seriousness as a theophany."
**Rule:** no list of exactly three grammatically parallel items without a deliberate reason. If you catch yourself writing one, cut to one item or expand to a sentence with unequal clause lengths.

### 2.4 Abstraction before image
**Bad:** "an intensity of existence normally inaccessible" / "a passionate movement of the soul toward the truth it desires."
**Why it's slop:** these are Latinate abstractions stacked on abstractions — technically meaningful, sensorially dead. Compare to the source material itself (burning bush, radio tuning to a frequency) which is always more alive than the connective prose around it.
**Rebuild:** ground the abstraction in a concrete noun or action before or instead of naming it.
> Bad: "a dynamic field of personified forces"
> Good: "a crowded middle world — gods bumping into daimons, none of them metaphors"
**Rule:** every paragraph needs at least one noun you could touch, see, or hear. If a paragraph is 100% abstract nouns, stop and insert an image.

### 2.5 Hedge-everything qualifiers
**Bad:** "seems," "in some mediated or indirect way," "however far removed," "to some extent," "arguably."
**Why it's slop:** it's insurance against being wrong, and it flattens conviction into mush. Real essayists commit to a claim and let the *next* sentence complicate it — they don't pre-complicate the first one.
**Rebuild:** state the claim flat. If you need to hedge, hedge in a *separate* sentence, visibly, as a turn — not baked into the clause.
> Bad: "then it participates in the intelligible, however far removed."
> Good: "then it participates in the intelligible. Uncomfortable, but there it is."
**Rule:** max one hedge word per paragraph. Locate your hedges after drafting and cut at least half.

### 2.6 The even pulse
**Bad:** every sentence in a paragraph running 15–25 words, same subordinate-clause architecture, no rhythm variation.
**Why it's slop:** prose with no heartbeat reads as generated even when every individual sentence is fine.
**Rebuild:** after drafting a paragraph, mark word counts. If three sentences in a row are within 5 words of each other, break one violently short or let one run long and breathless.
**Rule:** at least one sentence under 6 words per 150 words of prose. At least one sentence that runs past 35 words, if the paragraph is long enough to earn it.

### 2.7 Restating the quote you just gave
**Bad:** quote something that makes a claim, then the next sentence explains the same claim in flatter words.
**Why it's slop:** it's padding that looks like analysis. If the quote already said it, the next sentence needs to do new work — extend it, argue with it, or place it against something else.
**Rule:** after any quotation or paraphrase of a source, the following sentence must add a fact, a comparison, or a judgment — never a restatement.

