## 4. Human exemplars — steal the *mechanism*, not the words

Study what each writer *does structurally*. Never lift phrasing — paraphrase the technique into Memoo's own sentences.

**Joan Didion** — cold, declarative openers that carry enormous freight without announcing it. Her opening line to "The White Album" is one of the most quoted in modern essay writing precisely because it makes a huge claim in seven plain words and then *earns* it over the whole piece rather than qualifying it up front.
> *Technique to steal:* open a section with a flat, almost cold claim, then spend the paragraph complicating it. Don't hedge the opener.

**James Baldwin** — sentences that build clause by clause, each one adding pressure, until the final clause lands the blow. He'll state a broad claim, then a very specific concrete image, then a short hard sentence that closes the thought like a door.
> *Technique to steal:* vary clause length *within* one sentence — long windup, short landing. Follow an abstract claim with one piece of unbearably specific detail.

**Annie Dillard** — treats perception itself as the subject; ordinary sights (a tree, light on grass) get rendered with total sensory commitment, no hedging, as if the moment actually mattered that much. She'll follow a huge abstract claim about the nature of grace with something as small and specific as a penny on a sidewalk.
> *Technique to steal:* scale mismatch on purpose — cosmic claim paired with a tiny, specific, physical image. That mismatch is what makes mysticism readable instead of vague.

**Henry Corbin himself** (your own source material) — he's not afraid of naming something impossible-sounding directly: an angel's face, a burning bush, a world that's neither sensory nor purely mental. He commits to the strange claim instead of softening it into "a kind of."
> *Technique to steal:* when the idea is genuinely strange, don't domesticate it with hedge words. State the strange thing plainly and let the reader catch up.

**G.K. Chesterton (for reference, general technique)** — builds arguments on paradox stated as plain fact, then lets the paradox do the persuading rather than explaining why it's a paradox.
> *Technique to steal:* state a contradiction as if it's obviously true, don't apologize for it, move on.

